= [+ date_label +] — Two weeks, one studio, one former president

They put him on between the rounds because they always put him on between the
rounds. **Aleksander Kwaśniewski** is the only guest a Polish news channel can
book who has personally won this election, and he is asked, inevitably, about
**[+ pres_2025_left_candidate +]**, who took **[+ pres_2025_player_result +]%**
— [+ pres_2025_candidate_band +].

[? if kwasniewski_2025_tone = "vindicated": He calls it the best presidential
campaign the Polish left has run since his own re-election, and says so in the
tone of a man who has been waiting two decades to be able to say it about
somebody else.?]
[? if kwasniewski_2025_tone = "warm": He says the candidacy finally stopped
apologising for existing, which from him is not faint praise; he spent 2015 and
2020 watching left candidates apologise.?]
[? if kwasniewski_2025_tone = "elegiac": He is kind about it, which is worse
than being harsh. The campaign, he says, deserved better than the country gave
it — and then he moves, unhurried, to what happens in the second round.?]

Then he does the thing he was actually booked to do. He says that a runoff
between the democratic camp and the right is not an occasion for the left to
demonstrate its independence, that he has heard that argument before, and that
he can name the year it cost the country a presidency.

- @runoff_bloc
- @runoff_price
- @runoff_ignore

@runoff_bloc
