    "Kwaśniewski spends the fortnight campaigning for the democratic finalist in towns the Left cannot reach";
!}

He takes the small towns, the pensioners' associations and the local radio
stations, and he talks to the electorate that stopped voting for anybody after
2015 — his electorate, the one nobody in our campaign has ever been able to
telephone. He does it for two weeks without asking us for anything.

Razem's organisers are entitled to their view of what a former president who
sent troops to Iraq is doing on our platform. It is a real objection, and it does
not move a single vote in Radom.

- @poland_event_queue: Let the fortnight run

@runoff_price
title: Make him extract something written before he says another word
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource. He is the only person both finalists' camps will still take a telephone call from; spend that rather than his warmth.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: There is no negotiating team to send with him.
on-arrival: {!
Q.resources -= 1;
Q.kwasniewski_standing += 4;
Q.kwasniewski_alignment_bias += 4;
Q.negotiation_capital += 5;
Q.ko_relation += 4;
Q.constitutional_restraint += 3;
Q.institutional_trust += 2;
Q.pres_2025_transfer_strength += 2;
Q.public_trust += 1;
Q.razem_dissent += 2;
Q.news_headline =
    "A former president extracts a written commitment from the democratic finalist before endorsing";
!}

We send lawyers with him. He is the one intermediary in the country whose call
the finalist's chief of staff will take inside the hour during a runoff week,
and he uses it exactly as we ask: nothing about ministries, everything about
what a president signs, refers and refuses in the first year.

The text is short and it exists. Two weeks of goodwill would have evaporated by
July; a paragraph initialled by a president-elect does not.

- @poland_event_queue: Bank the text

@runoff_ignore
title: Thank him and run our own fortnight
subtitle: Free. No borrowed authority, no borrowed obligations, and no answer to the question he asked on television.
on-arrival: {!
Q.kwasniewski_standing -= 1;
Q.kwasniewski_alignment_bias -= 3;
Q.progressive_credibility += 2;
Q.party_unity += 2;
Q.razem_dissent -= 3;
Q.pensioner_support -= 1;
Q.ko_relation -= 2;
Q.news_headline =
    "The Left runs its own runoff fortnight without a former president's help";
!}

We thank him for the kind words about the campaign and decline the offer behind
them. The fortnight is ours to spend, and it is spent on our own organisers,
our own arguments and our own electorate.

He does not sulk. He simply answers the next interviewer's question about the
left's runoff strategy honestly, which is the most expensive thing he could
possibly do.

- @poland_event_queue: Run our own fortnight


@broker
# prose-audit: Grade B — The brokerage is bounded and expensive: he can make one agreement between democratic parties easier, once, and each route names what the intermediary costs as well as what he unblocks.
title: At the President's office
subtitle: The democratic parties cannot agree, and one of them has already asked a man with no office to sit in the room
face-image: img/poland/events/aleksander-kwasniewski-sejm-2025.webp
