order: 6
max-visits: 1
view-if: continuous_campaign = 1 and kwasniewski_channel = 1 and kwasniewski_estranged = 0 and kwasniewski_alignment >= 45 and kwasniewski_broker_done = 0 and year >= 2023 and (government_coalition_dissent >= 30 or ko_relation <= 34 or opposition_list_pressure >= 3)
on-arrival: {!
Q.kwasniewski_broker_done = 1;
const dissent = Number(Q.government_coalition_dissent) || 0;
const listPressure = Number(Q.opposition_list_pressure) || 0;
// One room, three possible subjects, chosen by whichever thing is actually
// breaking. Nothing new is invented: each subject is a live quarrel the
// democratic camp is already having elsewhere in the game.
Q.kwasniewski_broker_subject =
    Q.left_in_government && dissent >= 30
        ? "coalition"
        : (listPressure >= 3 ? "lists" : "camp");
Q.news_headline =
    "A former president turns out to have been in the room the whole time";
!}

= [+ date_label +] — He was already there

The meeting is at somebody else's foundation, about somebody else's report, and
the invitation is worded to make attendance look optional. Our delegation
arrives, is shown into a room with bad coffee, and finds **Aleksander
Kwaśniewski** already sitting at the end of the table with a folder, because of
course he is.

[? if kwasniewski_broker_subject = "coalition": Nobody in this cabinet can
convene the other parties in it without the meeting itself becoming a story
about who convened it. He can. He has no ministry to protect, no list to
negotiate and no successor to promote, and that is now the scarcest quality in
the coalition.?]
[? if kwasniewski_broker_subject = "lists": The list question has reached the
stage where every party's negotiator is briefing against every other party's
negotiator, and nobody can propose a compromise without it being read as
weakness. A man with no candidacy can propose anything.?]
[? if kwasniewski_broker_subject = "camp": The democratic camp is not
negotiating so much as accumulating grievances in parallel. He has invited the
people who actually decide, given them one afternoon, and declined to tell the
press what it is about until it is over.?]

He is explicit about his own limits, twice, unprompted. He cannot deliver a
single vote. He cannot promise anything on anyone's behalf. What he can do is
make it slightly embarrassing for a serious person to walk out of the room, and
he has been doing that professionally since Bill Clinton was in office.

- @broker_use
- @broker_host
- @broker_decline

@broker_use
title: Let him broker it, and take the agreement
subtitle: The quickest route to a deal the parties have been unable to reach alone; the credit belongs to the room, not to us.
on-arrival: {!
Q.kwasniewski_standing += 3;
Q.kwasniewski_alignment_bias += 5;
Q.ko_relation += 6;
Q.p2050_relation += 4;
Q.psl_relation += 3;
Q.negotiation_capital += 6;
Q.government_coalition_dissent -= 10;
Q.opposition_list_pressure -= 2;
Q.public_trust += 2;
Q.institutional_trust += 2;
Q.razem_dissent += 3;
Q.progressives_dissent += 2;
Q.news_headline =
    "A quarrel between democratic parties is settled in a room a former president booked";
!}

It takes one afternoon and it works, which is infuriating. The substance is
ours — our text, our numbers, our concession schedule — and the reason it is
signed today rather than in November is that every person in the room would
rather concede than be the one who made Kwaśniewski's afternoon a failure.

The agreement holds. The story is about him. Both of those are the deal.

- @poland_event_queue: Take the agreement

@broker_host
title: Host the next one ourselves, with him in the room
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource. Slower and less certain, but the convening power ends up in our building rather than his foundation's.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: We cannot finance a standing convening operation.
on-arrival: {!
Q.resources -= 1;
Q.kwasniewski_standing += 2;
Q.kwasniewski_alignment_bias += 3;
Q.ko_relation += 3;
Q.p2050_relation += 2;
Q.psl_relation += 2;
Q.negotiation_capital += 3;
Q.government_coalition_dissent -= 5;
Q.opposition_list_pressure -= 1;
Q.media_capacity += 2;
Q.institutional_trust += 2;
Q.leadership_authority += 3;
Q.news_headline =
    "Lewica takes over the convening role a former president had been performing for the democratic camp";
!}

We ask him to come to us next time, and to say publicly that he came to us. It
is a smaller agreement than the one he could have brokered alone — two parties
leave with things unsettled — but the room is ours, the invitation list is ours,
and the second meeting does not require his diary at all.

He agrees immediately and without vanity. He has spent twenty years watching
people try to inherit this function and fail, and he is genuinely curious
whether we can.

- @poland_event_queue: Host the second meeting

@broker_decline
title: Refuse to negotiate through an intermediary nobody elected
subtitle: Free. A principled and entirely defensible answer that leaves the quarrel exactly where it was.
on-arrival: {!
Q.kwasniewski_standing -= 2;
Q.kwasniewski_alignment_bias -= 4;
Q.progressive_credibility += 3;
Q.party_unity += 2;
Q.razem_dissent -= 3;
Q.government_coalition_dissent += 3;
Q.ko_relation -= 3;
Q.public_trust -= 1;
Q.news_headline =
    "Lewica refuses to settle a political quarrel through a former president's foundation";
!}

Our delegation says what is true: that agreements between parties belong in
parties, that the practice of settling politics in private rooms convened by
retired officeholders is precisely what a generation of voters despises, and
that we will negotiate in the coalition council like everybody else.

Everyone in the room agrees with the principle. Nobody in the room signs
anything. The quarrel continues at its own pace, and the next attempt to fix it
will cost us something we would rather have spent elsewhere.

- @poland_event_queue: Negotiate in the ordinary way


@road_sign
# prose-audit: Grade A — The last flashing sign before the party leaves the normal left. He cannot stop Miller, cannot depose him and cannot reach the ballot deadline; the three answers are partial reversal, an honest generational refusal, or a permanent break that helps the transformation and ends him.
title: The former president asks Leszek Miller what he thinks he is doing
subtitle: He cannot stop this and knows it. He intends to be on the record anyway.
face-image: img/poland/events/aleksander-kwasniewski-lewica-2025.webp
new-page: true
tags: poland_event, poland_event_calendar
priority: 34
order: 5
max-visits: 1
view-if: continuous_campaign = 1 and sld_populist_route_active = 1 and sld_populist_orientation != "unsettled" and sld_populist_orientation != "none" and kwasniewski_estranged = 0 and kwasniewski_roadsign_done = 0
on-arrival: {!
Q.kwasniewski_roadsign_done = 1;
Q.kwasniewski_channel = 1;
Q.kwasniewski_verdict =
    "Publicly disowned the social-populist refoundation of the party";
Q.news_headline =
    "Kwaśniewski says the party he built no longer exists and names what replaced it";
!}

= [+ date_label +] — The last road sign

He does not ask for a meeting. He gives an interview, and it is the longest one
he has given about Polish domestic politics in a decade.

**Aleksander Kwaśniewski** and **Leszek Miller** built the same party. They ran
the same governments, took the country into the same European Union, and spent
2003 and 2004 destroying each other over who was responsible for what it had
become. He is therefore the one person in Poland who cannot be accused of
sentimentality about Miller, and the one person whose objection Miller cannot
dismiss as coming from someone who was never there.

He is asked about **[+ sld_populist_ideology +]**.
[? if sld_populist_orientation = "konfederacja": He is asked, specifically,
about a conditional pact with Konfederacja. He answers that he negotiated
Poland's accession treaty with people who now sit in that federation's
parliamentary club, that he knows exactly what they think of it, and that there
is no welfare programme in Europe large enough to make that trade rational.?]
[? if sld_populist_orientation = "pis": He is asked, specifically, about
tactical support for PiS. He answers that he spent his second term defending an
independent judiciary against people who are now offering us protected list
places, and that a left which accepts them has not outmanoeuvred anybody.?]
[? if sld_populist_orientation = "independent": He is asked about the
independent campaign, and is more careful, because it is the least indefensible
of the three. He still calls it Samoobrona with better historians, and he still
says the word out loud.?]
[? if sld_populist_escalation = "anti-vaccine conspiratorial mobilisation": He
is then asked about the vaccine campaign, and stops being careful at all.?]

He has no power here. He cannot call a congress, discipline a chairman or reach
the electoral commission. What he has is every camera in the country and the
knowledge that whatever we say next will be quoted at our candidates in every
district until the ballot.

- @road_sign_accommodate
- @road_sign_decline
- @road_sign_break

@road_sign_accommodate
title: Concede ground and pull part of the transformation back
subtitle: Cost: 2 resources. Restore the European and constitutional planks and reopen the progressive settlement; the rural project keeps its organisation but loses its edge.
choose-if: resources >= 2
unavailable-subtitle: Reversing a refoundation in public costs two resources we do not have.
